1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,520
A soil sensor in a field in Jhapa notices the ground is dry.

2
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:14,259
Its reading hops over the mobile network to a server in a data centre, a trained model decides the crop needs water, and the farmer's phone switches the pump on.

3
00:00:14,259 --> 00:00:16,595
Ten seconds, no human in the loop.

4
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:26,460
That one moment uses almost every topic in this unit: the internet of things, mobile computing, cloud computing and artificial intelligence.

5
00:00:31,933 --> 00:00:37,989
Welcome to Class 12 Computer Science, Unit 7: Recent Trends in Technology.

6
00:00:37,989 --> 00:00:43,456
Numbers in this unit date fast, so whenever you quote one in the exam, quote its year too.

7
00:00:45,833 --> 00:00:46,967
Six parts.

8
00:00:46,967 --> 00:00:49,598
Artificial intelligence and robotics.

9
00:00:49,598 --> 00:00:50,900
Cloud computing.

10
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:51,915
Big data.

11
00:00:51,915 --> 00:00:53,374
Virtual reality.

12
00:00:53,374 --> 00:00:56,642
e-commerce, e-medicine and e-governance.

13
00:00:56,642 --> 00:00:59,231
And mobile computing with the internet of things.

14
00:01:03,567 --> 00:01:14,571
Artificial intelligence is building machines that do jobs which would need human intelligence: learning, reasoning, understanding language, recognising patterns.

15
00:01:14,571 --> 00:01:21,147
Inside it sits machine learning, which learns patterns from data instead of following hand written rules.

16
00:01:21,147 --> 00:01:25,613
Inside that, deep learning, with many layered neural networks.

17
00:01:25,613 --> 00:01:35,822
And inside that, the newest layer: generative AI, the large language models behind ChatGPT, which create new text, code and pictures.

18
00:01:36,033 --> 00:01:37,649
A few dates worth knowing.

19
00:01:37,649 --> 00:01:41,009
The term was coined at Dartmouth in nineteen fifty six.

20
00:01:41,009 --> 00:01:54,128
Deep Blue beat Kasparov at chess in ninety seven, AlphaGo won at Go in twenty sixteen, the transformer was published in twenty seventeen, and ChatGPT arrived in November twenty twenty two.

21
00:01:55,333 --> 00:02:00,230
By capability, AI is narrow, general or super.

22
00:02:00,230 --> 00:02:08,095
Narrow AI does one task well, and every system in use today is narrow, however clever it looks.

23
00:02:08,095 --> 00:02:15,414
General AI, human level at everything, does not yet exist; super AI is hypothetical.

24
00:02:16,633 --> 00:02:26,192
Robotics designs, builds and programs robots: machines that sense their surroundings, process what they sense, and act on the physical world.

25
00:02:26,192 --> 00:02:36,270
Sensors feed the controller; the controller's program decides; actuators, the motors, move the end effector, the gripper or wheels.

26
00:02:36,270 --> 00:02:40,077
And the sensors then see the result, closing the feedback loop.

27
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:56,966
Robots by type: industrial arms, mobile warehouse robots, service robots like the robot waiter a Kathmandu startup built, surgical robots, drones that mapped the damage after the twenty fifteen earthquake, and explorers like the Mars rovers.

28
00:02:56,966 --> 00:03:02,617
AI and robots work without tiring, fast and safely in danger.

29
00:03:02,617 --> 00:03:10,193
But they cost a lot, displace jobs, lack common sense, and a model trained on biased data gives biased answers.

30
00:03:14,967 --> 00:03:22,130
Cloud computing delivers computing services over the internet, on demand, and you pay only for what you use.

31
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:26,583
The provider owns and maintains the hardware; you rent capacity.

32
00:03:26,583 --> 00:03:39,135
The official NIST definition lists five characteristics: self service on demand, access over the network, pooled resources shared by many customers, elasticity, and metered pay per use.

33
00:03:39,135 --> 00:03:41,569
The trick underneath is virtualisation.

34
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:46,942
The three service models differ in how much of the stack you still manage.

35
00:03:46,942 --> 00:03:52,059
On your own premises, you manage everything, down to the network cables.

36
00:03:52,059 --> 00:03:59,104
Infrastructure as a service rents you virtual machines: you still install the operating system and software.

37
00:03:59,104 --> 00:04:04,677
Platform as a service gives a ready platform: you bring only your application and data.

38
00:04:04,677 --> 00:04:11,504
Software as a service, like Gmail, gives finished software: you manage nothing but your own data.

39
00:04:12,733 --> 00:04:14,245
Examples to quote.

40
00:04:14,245 --> 00:04:17,487
Infrastructure: Amazon EC2.

41
00:04:17,487 --> 00:04:19,675
Platform: Google App Engine.

42
00:04:19,675 --> 00:04:22,813
Software: Gmail and Google Docs.

43
00:04:22,813 --> 00:04:29,979
And by who may use it, a cloud is public, private to one organisation, shared by a community, or a hybrid.

44
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:32,531
Cloud or your own server?

45
00:04:32,733 --> 00:04:37,498
Spread the hardware over sixty months: seven thousand five hundred a month.

46
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:44,561
Three hundred watts all month is two hundred and sixteen units: two thousand three hundred and seventy six rupees.

47
00:04:44,767 --> 00:04:48,794
With the admin's time, eleven thousand eight hundred and seventy six a month.

48
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:56,559
So the cloud saves about two thousand nine hundred a month, thirty four and a half thousand a year, before counting the UPS and load shedding.

49
00:04:58,567 --> 00:05:02,136
The costs: no internet, no service.

50
00:05:02,136 --> 00:05:03,803
The bill never ends.

51
00:05:03,803 --> 00:05:08,517
Your data may physically sit in another country, under its laws.

52
00:05:08,517 --> 00:05:11,251
Vendor lock in makes leaving expensive.

53
00:05:11,251 --> 00:05:14,402
And when the provider goes down, so do you.

54
00:05:14,402 --> 00:05:21,521
As of twenty twenty five, Amazon held about thirty per cent of the market, Microsoft twenty two and Google twelve.

55
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:34,440
Big data is data so large, so fast, or so varied that ordinary database tools cannot handle it in time.

56
00:05:34,440 --> 00:05:37,265
The standard answer is the five Vs.

57
00:05:37,265 --> 00:05:39,518
Volume, the sheer quantity.

58
00:05:39,518 --> 00:05:42,278
Velocity, the speed it arrives.

59
00:05:42,278 --> 00:05:46,901
Variety, tables mixed with logs, photos and voice.

60
00:05:46,901 --> 00:05:49,440
Veracity, how much of it is wrong.

61
00:05:49,440 --> 00:05:52,060
And value, the insight you can extract.

62
00:05:53,267 --> 00:05:54,128
How big?

63
00:05:54,128 --> 00:06:05,664
Estimates put the data created in twenty twenty five at around a hundred and eighty zettabytes, nearly three times twenty twenty, roughly doubling every two to three years.

64
00:06:05,664 --> 00:06:09,141
One zettabyte is ten to the twenty one bytes.

65
00:06:09,141 --> 00:06:17,991
No single server copes, so big data runs on distributed tools: Hadoop, Spark, and NoSQL databases such as MongoDB.

66
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:19,976
Is it big data?

67
00:06:20,433 --> 00:06:24,546
Seventeen thousand two hundred and eighty readings per sensor per day.

68
00:06:24,767 --> 00:06:30,840
Times two thousand sensors, times eighty bytes: about two point seven six gigabytes a day.

69
00:06:31,067 --> 00:06:32,905
About one terabyte a year.

70
00:06:32,905 --> 00:06:36,092
Volume alone, an ordinary database could store.

71
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:43,411
But four hundred readings arrive every second, and a flood warning computed tomorrow is useless.

72
00:06:43,411 --> 00:06:46,833
Velocity, plus variety and veracity, make it a genuine big data problem.

